export const modernPerspective = [
  'The most important validation problem in ab initio molecular dynamics is layered. Electronic self-consistency controls the instantaneous force, the nuclear time step controls integration error, thermostats or barostats define the sampled ensemble, and finite trajectory length controls statistical uncertainty. Convergence of one layer does not validate the others.',
  'Classical nuclei are often adequate for heavy atoms and high temperatures, but light nuclei can require nuclear quantum treatments. Path-integral approaches change the treatment of the nuclei while leaving the electronic force problem as a separate approximation layer; they should not be confused with the word “quantum” in Martin’s QMD terminology.',
  'Long trajectories are correlated data. Structural averages, diffusion coefficients, free energies, and transport observables require equilibration and sampling analysis rather than a count of stored frames. A stable-looking trajectory is therefore evidence of numerical propagation, not by itself evidence of thermodynamic equilibrium.',
  'Machine-learned interatomic potentials can extend the time and length scales accessible after training on electronic-structure data, but they replace repeated electronic-force evaluations with a fitted model. Their transferability and uncertainty are separate questions from the convergence of the electronic calculations used to generate the reference data.',
];

export const synthesis =
  'Chapter 19 turns the static energy landscape of electronic structure into motion. Born–Oppenheimer molecular dynamics repeatedly solves the electronic ground state and then advances classical nuclei with the resulting forces; Car–Parrinello instead introduces fictitious electronic dynamics so that orbital optimization and nuclear motion evolve together while remaining close to the adiabatic surface. Plane waves and FFTs make the repeated Hamiltonian operations efficient, while approximate non-self-consistent models trade electronic fidelity for speed. The examples show why the method matters: it can connect electronic bonding to liquids, melting, reactions, and finite-temperature material behavior. The next chapter takes a complementary route—rather than following large nuclear motion in time, it asks how a system responds to small perturbations, leading to phonons, dielectric response, electron–phonon coupling, and magnons.';
---

<section aria-labelledby="section-19-1-forces">
  <h2 id="section-19-1-forces">19.1 Molecular Dynamics (MD): Forces from the Electrons</h2>
  <p>
    Quantum molecular dynamics begins with ordinary classical mechanics for the nuclei. The electronic problem enters through the potential-energy surface on which those nuclei move. If the nuclear coordinates are <em>R</em><sub>I</sub> and the electronic ground-state energy at those coordinates is <em>E</em>, Newton's equation is
  </p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><msub><mover><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="bold">F</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi><mi>I</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_I&#92;ddot&#123;&#92;mathbf R&#125;_I=&#92;mathbf F_I=-&#92;frac&#123;&#92;partial E&#125;&#123;&#92;partial &#92;mathbf R_I&#125;</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>
    The adjective <em>quantum</em> in Martin's terminology refers to how the force is obtained: the electrons are treated quantum mechanically, usually through Kohn–Sham density functional theory, while the nuclei in this chapter are propagated as classical particles. This is the <a href={href('/theory/classical-mechanics/')}>classical nuclear dynamics</a> built on the Born–Oppenheimer energy surface, not a fully quantum simulation of nuclear motion.
  </p>
  <p>
    A numerical trajectory replaces continuous time by finite steps. Martin uses the Verlet update to expose the basic structure:
  </p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block">
      <semantics>
        <mrow>
          <msub><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>Δt</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>Δt</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>+</mo>
          <mfrac><msup><mi>Δt</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>I</mi></msub></mfrac><msub><mi mathvariant="bold">F</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>)</mo>
        </mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&#92;mathbf R_I(t+&#92;Delta t)=2&#92;mathbf R_I(t)-&#92;mathbf R_I(t-&#92;Delta t)+&#92;frac&#123;(&#92;Delta t)^2&#125;&#123;M_I&#125;&#92;mathbf F_I(t)</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>
    The time step must resolve the fastest relevant nuclear motion. A symplectic-like integrator such as Verlet can remain stable for long runs when the step is appropriate, but a finite time step still creates integration error. Energy conservation in a microcanonical trajectory is therefore a diagnostic of the combined force and integration accuracy, not a proof that the electronic approximation is physically correct.
  </p>
  <p>
    For Kohn–Sham QMD the energy depends on both the orbitals and nuclear coordinates. Martin writes it schematically as
  </p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>[</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>]</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>N</mi></munderover><mo>⟨</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>|</mo><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><msup><mo>∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>⟩</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>]</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>I</mi><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E[&#123;&#92;psi_i&#125;,&#123;&#92;mathbf R_I&#125;]=2&#92;sum_i^N&#92;langle&#92;psi_i|-&#92;frac12&#92;nabla^2|&#92;psi_i&#92;rangle+U[n]+E_&#123;II&#125;</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>
    where <em>U</em>[<em>n</em>] contains the electron–ion, Hartree, and exchange–correlation contributions. When the electronic state is sufficiently converged, the force theorem supplies the derivative needed for nuclear motion. The force therefore inherits every approximation in the underlying electronic calculation: the exchange–correlation functional, core treatment, basis or grid, Brillouin-zone sampling when relevant, and the quality of <a href={href('/theory/self-consistent-field-methods/')}>self-consistency</a>.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="section-19-2-born-oppenheimer">
  <h2 id="section-19-2-born-oppenheimer">19.2 Born-Oppenheimer Molecular Dynamics</h2>
  <p>
    Born–Oppenheimer molecular dynamics separates each time step into two problems. First, hold the nuclei fixed and solve the electronic ground state accurately enough to obtain reliable forces. Second, use those forces to advance the nuclei with an ordinary molecular-dynamics integrator. The electronic minimization is therefore repeated along a sequence of nearby geometries.
  </p>
  <p>
    This separation explains both the strength and the cost of the method. The nuclear time step is chosen from nuclear frequencies, so it can be relatively long compared with an electronic time scale. However, the electronic state must be reconverged at every step. Martin stresses that forces can require substantially tighter electronic convergence than energies because the variational property protects the energy more strongly than its derivative.
  </p>
  <p>
    The electronic solver does not always need individually converged eigenvectors. For an insulator, operations on the occupied subspace can be more efficient because the density and total energy are invariant under unitary rotations among occupied states. Metals are more delicate: fractional Fermi occupations make the eigenvalue spectrum near the chemical potential directly relevant, so occupation and smearing choices enter the force calculation as additional numerical and physical assumptions.
  </p>
  <p>
    Consecutive MD configurations are close, which makes the previous density, potential, or orbitals useful starting guesses for the next electronic solve. This can greatly reduce iterative work, but the extrapolation must not introduce a systematic lag or bias. If the electronic error correlates with the direction of motion, the resulting force error can accumulate as a drift in the nominally conserved energy. The important distinction is therefore between a fast initial guess and the final force accuracy actually delivered at each step.
  </p>
  <p>
    In compact form, Born–Oppenheimer MD is a nested loop:
  </p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>→</mo><mi>SCF</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>min</mi><mo>→</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="bold">F</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>→</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&#92;mathbf R_n&#92;rightarrow &#92;mathrm&#123;SCF/minimization&#125;&#92;rightarrow E_n,&#92;mathbf F_n&#92;rightarrow &#92;mathbf R_&#123;n+1&#125;</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>
    This is the same electronic force problem used in structural relaxation, now embedded inside a time integrator. That connection is why the numerical disciplines established earlier in the book—basis convergence, force consistency, SCF stability, and efficient iterative solution—become prerequisites for trustworthy dynamics rather than separate topics.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="section-19-3-car-parrinello">
  <h2 id="section-19-3-car-parrinello">19.3 Car–Parrinello Unified Algorithm for Electrons and Ions</h2>
  <p>
    Car and Parrinello reorganized the problem. Instead of completing an electronic ground-state calculation before every nuclear step, they introduced dynamics for the electronic orbitals themselves. Nuclear coordinates and Kohn–Sham orbitals then evolve together in one constrained dynamical system, with the electronic degrees of freedom designed to remain close to the instantaneous Born–Oppenheimer minimum.
  </p>
  <p>
    The construction starts from a fictitious Lagrangian. In addition to the physical ionic kinetic energy and the Kohn–Sham potential energy, it assigns the orbitals a fictitious mass <em>μ</em> and kinetic energy, while Lagrange multipliers preserve orbital orthonormality:
  </p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block">
      <semantics>
        <mrow>
          <mi>ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>μ</mi><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>N</mi></munderover><mo>⟨</mo><msub><mover><mi>ψ</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>|</mo><msub><mover><mi>ψ</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>⟩</mo><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mi>I</mi><mi>N</mi></munderover><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><msubsup><mover><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>I</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>+</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi></mi></munderover><msub><mi>Λ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>(</mo><mo>⟨</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>⟩</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo>
        </mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&#92;mathcal L=&#92;mu&#92;sum_i&#92;langle&#92;dot&#123;&#92;psi&#125;_i|&#92;dot&#123;&#92;psi&#125;_i&#92;rangle+&#92;frac12&#92;sum_I M_I&#92;dot&#123;&#92;mathbf R&#125;_I^2-E+&#92;sum_&#123;ij&#125;&#92;Lambda_&#123;ij&#125;(&#92;langle&#92;psi_i|&#92;psi_j&#92;rangle-&#92;delta_&#123;ij&#125;)</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>
    The precise factor multiplying the electronic kinetic term is a convention; the essential new parameter is the fictitious electronic mass. Variation gives coupled second-order equations,
  </p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block">
      <semantics>
        <mrow>
          <mi>μ</mi><msub><mover><mi>ψ</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mover><mi>H</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mi>k</mi><mi></mi></munderover><msub><mi>Λ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1em"/><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><msub><mover><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi><mi>I</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac>
        </mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&#92;mu&#92;ddot&#123;&#92;psi&#125;_i=-&#92;hat H&#92;psi_i+&#92;sum_k&#92;Lambda_&#123;ik&#125;&#92;psi_k,&#92;qquad M_I&#92;ddot&#123;&#92;mathbf R&#125;_I=-&#92;frac&#123;&#92;partial E&#125;&#123;&#92;partial &#92;mathbf R_I&#125;</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>
    When all fictitious velocities vanish, the electronic equation becomes
  </p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><mover><mi>H</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mi>k</mi><mi></mi></munderover><msub><mi>Λ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&#92;hat H&#92;psi_i=&#92;sum_k&#92;Lambda_&#123;ik&#125;&#92;psi_k</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>
    which is the usual variational Kohn–Sham condition expressed for the occupied subspace. Diagonalizing the Hermitian multiplier matrix recovers ordinary Kohn–Sham eigenstates and eigenvalues. This is why dynamical quenching of the fictitious electronic kinetic energy can also be used as an optimization method for finding a ground state.
  </p>
  <p>
    The fictitious dynamics is only an optimization device. It is not the physical time evolution of the electrons and cannot be interpreted as an electronic excitation spectrum. Its success depends on adiabatic separation: the fictitious electronic oscillations should remain much faster than nuclear motion. Martin's two-level discussion gives the scale
  </p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><mfrac><mi>ΔE</mi><mi>μ</mi></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1em"/><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>≫</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>ion</mi></mrow></msub></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&#92;omega_e^2&#92;sim&#92;frac&#123;&#92;Delta E&#125;&#123;&#92;mu&#125;,&#92;qquad &#92;omega_e&#92;gg&#92;omega_&#123;&#92;mathrm&#123;ion&#125;&#125;</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>
    so the useful choice of <em>μ</em> depends on the electronic gap and the represented spectrum. This immediately exposes the difficult cases. If levels cross or a metal has arbitrarily small electronic gaps, clean frequency separation can fail and fictitious electronic energy can couple to nuclear motion. A smaller <em>μ</em> improves separation but pushes the electronic frequencies upward and forces a shorter integration step. Car–Parrinello dynamics therefore contains a genuine numerical trade-off absent from the Born–Oppenheimer separation.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="section-19-4-plane-waves">
  <h2 id="section-19-4-plane-waves">19.4 Expressions for Plane Waves</h2>
  <p>
    Martin uses plane waves to show why the Car–Parrinello idea became computationally practical. At the Brillouin-zone center an orbital can be expanded as
  </p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">G</mi><mi></mi></munderover><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">G</mi><mo>)</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mi>Ω</mi></msqrt></mfrac><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">G</mi><mo>·</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_i(&#92;mathbf r)=&#92;sum_&#123;&#92;mathbf G&#125;c_i(&#92;mathbf G)&#92;frac&#123;1&#125;&#123;&#92;sqrt&#123;&#92;Omega&#125;&#125;e^&#123;i&#92;mathbf G&#92;cdot&#92;mathbf r&#125;</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>
    with orthonormal coefficient vectors,
  </p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">G</mi><mi></mi></munderover><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>*</mo></msubsup><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">G</mi><mo>)</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">G</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&#92;sum_&#123;&#92;mathbf G&#125;c_i^*(&#92;mathbf G)c_j(&#92;mathbf G)=&#92;delta_&#123;ij&#125;</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>
    A literal dense multiplication by the plane-wave Hamiltonian would waste the simplicity of this representation. The crucial computational observation is the same one developed in Chapters 12–13: kinetic energy is diagonal in reciprocal space, while multiplication by a local potential is diagonal in real space. Fast Fourier transforms shuttle the orbital between the two representations, so applying <span class="math-inline"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>H</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>ψ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&#92;hat H&#92;psi</annotation></semantics></math></span> scales roughly as <em>N</em><sub>PW</sub> log <em>N</em><sub>PW</sub> instead of a dense <em>N</em><sub>PW</sub><sup>2</sup> matrix operation.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is one of the central algorithmic lessons of the chapter: Car–Parrinello did not become transformative merely because it introduced another equation of motion. It combined orbital optimization, nuclear dynamics, occupied-subspace operations, and the FFT structure of <a href={href('/theory/plane-wave-and-real-space-methods/')}>plane-wave calculations</a> so that the expensive operation was repeatedly applying the Hamiltonian rather than constructing and diagonalizing a dense matrix.
  </p>
  <p>
    High Fourier components also expose a limitation. Their kinetic energies grow as |<strong>G</strong>|², so the fastest fictitious electronic frequency increases as the plane-wave cutoff is raised. A basis cutoff that is harmless for static convergence can therefore tighten the stable Car–Parrinello time step. Martin discusses altered fictitious masses and special treatment of high-frequency components as ways to manage this problem. Ultrasoft pseudopotentials and PAW change the detailed equations but preserve the same overall organization, with additional nucleus-attached terms entering overlap, energy, and force expressions.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="section-19-5-non-self-consistent">
  <h2 id="section-19-5-non-self-consistent">19.5 Non-self-consistent QMD Methods</h2>
  <p>
    Full self-consistent Kohn–Sham forces are not the only way to generate a trajectory. Martin separates two faster approximations. In empirical tight binding, the Hamiltonian matrix elements are prescribed functions of atomic geometry; a small basis can make direct diagonalization inexpensive, and energies and forces can be evaluated rapidly as atoms move. The speed comes from replacing the first-principles electronic problem with a parameterized model, so transferability of the model becomes part of the physical approximation.
  </p>
  <p>
    A second class uses approximate non-self-consistent electronic Hamiltonians built from first-principles ingredients, for example superpositions of atomic-like potentials, together with a consistent approximate energy functional. These methods need not contain fitted empirical parameters, but they still omit part of the self-consistent rearrangement of the electronic density. Their approximation is therefore different from stopping an ordinary SCF calculation a little early: the Hamiltonian and energy model themselves have been changed deliberately to gain efficiency.
  </p>
  <p>
    This distinction matters when comparing trajectories. A shorter wall time does not imply the same Born–Oppenheimer surface has been sampled more efficiently. One must first identify which electronic energy surface each method defines, then ask whether the nuclear integration accurately follows that surface.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="section-19-6-examples">
  <h2 id="section-19-6-examples">19.6 Examples of Simulations</h2>
  <p>
    Martin closes with applications because QMD is valuable precisely when static structures are insufficient. The examples range from liquid iron at conditions relevant to Earth's core, to hydrogen-bonded water and aqueous chemistry, catalytic reactions, carbon melting at extreme pressure, clusters, and noncollinear magnetic structures. The common theme is that forces and electronic states are available along the same sequence of atomic configurations.
  </p>
  <p>
    The carbon example is especially instructive because several distinct routes to a phase boundary can be compared. One can infer a melting-line slope from thermodynamic relations, simulate coexisting solid and liquid and observe which phase grows, or compute free energies by thermodynamic integration to reference systems of known entropy. These are different estimators of a thermodynamic boundary; simply heating one small cell until its structure becomes disordered is not equivalent to any of them.
  </p>
  <p>
    The Clausius–Clapeyron relation summarizes why volume and entropy changes matter at coexistence:
  </p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>dP</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>dT</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>ΔS</mi><mi>ΔV</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>ΔH</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mi>ΔV</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&#92;frac&#123;dP&#125;&#123;dT&#125;=&#92;frac&#123;&#92;Delta S&#125;&#123;&#92;Delta V&#125;=&#92;frac&#123;&#92;Delta H&#125;&#123;T&#92;Delta V&#125;</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>
    This illustrates a general boundary that becomes important throughout Part V: an electronic total energy at one configuration is not yet a finite-temperature free energy. Entropy and sampling enter when the scientific question is a phase diagram, melting point, diffusion coefficient, or equilibrium population.
  </p>
  <p>
    QMD also permits electronic observables to be evaluated for thermally disordered configurations. Martin's liquid-carbon example distinguishes a time-averaged density of states from a transport calculation: a DOS that looks free-electron-like does not by itself prove metallic conduction. Conductivity must be calculated from the relevant response expression for each configuration and then averaged over the sampled ensemble; the dc limit is
  </p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><munder><mi>lim</mi><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mi>σ</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&#92;sigma_&#123;dc&#125;=&#92;lim_&#123;&#92;omega&#92;to0&#125;&#92;sigma(&#92;omega)</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>
    The water and reaction examples add another warning. QMD can reveal proton transfer, solvent rearrangement, or a reaction pathway because the bonding can change as the electronic state is recalculated. Yet reaction barriers and liquid structure can be highly sensitive to the exchange–correlation approximation. Dynamical detail does not remove model error; it makes that error propagate through every force and every sampled configuration.
  </p>
  <p>
